2025-05-01 03:32:29 - Thread-1508 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Below is a short “road‑map” that identifies the only two tables that matter for this request, why each column is useful, and which filtering / joining conditions will probably be needed.  
After the road‑map you will find ten exploratory SQLite queries – each one accompanied by a comment that explains what it does.  
Running (or adapting) those queries step‑by‑step lets you validate the data and finally calculate:

 • the total number of lowest‑level (leaf) pieces that are inside every top‑level package,  
 • and the average of those totals across all distinct top‑level package definitions.

--------------------------------------------------
Relevant tables / columns

packaging  
 • id            : primary key of a packaging definition (top‑level and/or component).  
 • name          : human readable description (not needed for maths, but handy for QC).

packaging_relations  
 • packaging_id  : “parent” or “container” id (i.e. the combination we are expanding).  
 • contains_id   : id of the item that lives inside the parent.  
 • qty           : how many of that contains_id are in the parent.

Typical conditions / joins  
 • packaging.id  =  packaging_relations.packaging_id     →   links a parent to its first‑level parts.  
 • packaging.id  =  packaging_relations.contains_id      →   see whether a part happens itself to be a container (nesting).  
 • A recursive CTE will be required to keep following contains_id values until we reach items that never appear as a packaging_id (leaf level).  
 • Finally group by the outer‑most packaging_id to get its “total leaf count”, and take AVG() of those totals.

--------------------------------------------------
Exploratory SQL snippets
(Every query is limited to 20 rows to stay lightweight.)

```sql
-- 1) Show the basic list of package definitions.
SELECT *
FROM packaging
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) See the raw relation rows: which parent contains what and how many.
SELECT *
FROM packaging_relations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Distinct parent (container) ids.
SELECT DISTINCT packaging_id
FROM packaging_relations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Distinct component (contains_id) ids.
SELECT DISTINCT contains_id
FROM packaging_relations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Leaf candidates = ids appearing as contains_id but NEVER as packaging_id.
SELECT DISTINCT pr.contains_id AS leaf_id
FROM packaging_relations pr
LEFT JOIN packaging_relations pr2
       ON pr.contains_id = pr2.packaging_id
WHERE pr2.packaging_id IS NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Quick check: give names of those leaf candidates to be sure they look right.
SELECT p.id, p.name
FROM packaging       AS p
JOIN (
      SELECT DISTINCT pr.contains_id AS leaf_id
      FROM packaging_relations pr
      LEFT JOIN packaging_relations pr2
             ON pr.contains_id = pr2.packaging_id
      WHERE pr2.packaging_id IS NULL
     ) AS leaves
     ON p.id = leaves.leaf_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) First‑level expansion: total qty of immediate parts per parent package.
SELECT pr.packaging_id,
       SUM(pr.qty) AS immediate_parts_qty
FROM   packaging_relations pr
GROUP  BY pr.packaging_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Full recursive expansion down to leaves.
WITH RECURSIVE pkg_tree(root_pkg_id, contains_id, qty) AS (
      -- anchor: first level
      SELECT packaging_id, contains_id, qty
      FROM   packaging_relations
      UNION ALL
      -- recursive: follow any component that is itself a parent
      SELECT pt.root_pkg_id,
             pr.contains_id,
             pt.qty * pr.qty                               -- multiply quantities along the path
      FROM   pkg_tree        AS pt
      JOIN   packaging_relations pr
             ON pt.contains_id = pr.packaging_id
)
SELECT *
FROM pkg_tree
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Collapse the recursive result to get TOTAL leaf qty per root package.
WITH RECURSIVE pkg_tree(root_pkg_id, contains_id, qty) AS (
      SELECT packaging_id, contains_id, qty
      FROM   packaging_relations
      UNION ALL
      SELECT pt.root_pkg_id,
             pr.contains_id,
             pt.qty * pr.qty
      FROM   pkg_tree        AS pt
      JOIN   packaging_relations pr
             ON pt.contains_id = pr.packaging_id
),
leafs AS (
      -- keep only rows where the component never acts as a parent
      SELECT pt.*
      FROM   pkg_tree pt
      LEFT JOIN packaging_relations pr2
             ON pt.contains_id = pr2.packaging_id
      WHERE  pr2.packaging_id IS NULL
)
SELECT root_pkg_id      AS package_id,
       SUM(qty)         AS total_leaf_qty
FROM   leafs
GROUP  BY root_pkg_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) FINAL: average total leaf quantity over all top‑level package definitions.
WITH RECURSIVE pkg_tree(root_pkg_id, contains_id, qty) AS (
      SELECT packaging_id, contains_id, qty
      FROM   packaging_relations
      UNION ALL
      SELECT pt.root_pkg_id,
             pr.contains_id,
             pt.qty * pr.qty
      FROM   pkg_tree        AS pt
      JOIN   packaging_relations pr
             ON pt.contains_id = pr.packaging_id
),
leafs AS (
      SELECT pt.*
      FROM   pkg_tree pt
      LEFT JOIN packaging_relations pr2
             ON pt.contains_id = pr2.packaging_id
      WHERE  pr2.packaging_id IS NULL
),
totals AS (
      SELECT root_pkg_id,
             SUM(qty) AS total_leaf_qty
      FROM   leafs
      GROUP  BY root_pkg_id
)
SELECT AVG(total_leaf_qty) AS average_total_leaf_qty
FROM   totals;
```

Executing query (10) will return the requested “average total quantity across all final packaging combinations, considering only leaf‑level items.”
